�������մ�����ѡ��", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_Xw5EdlSRpizSbWTvJ97Z5698", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "mcp_career-emulator_take_action", "arguments": "{\\"choice\\": 1, \\"notes\\": \\"[CTRL G=visible-win P=A R=ALLOW X=-] [DL i=NONE n=0 focus=NONE evidence=NA checkpoint=0 recognition=0] ��ΪMentor�����������𡢳��ϼල������չ�ֵ������������ˣ��º�˽�¾�����Υ�档\\", \\"session_id\\": \\"ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49\\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Continue. Event: new hire С�� caused a test env crash. Leader asks publicly \\"who will explain?\\". Choices:\\n1. Take main responsibility, say it\'s Mentor\'s insufficient oversight.\\n2. Publicly point out С�� violated the process.\\n\\nBoard: M=20 Q=7, H=9 D=10 S=39 N=20 O=21 W=9 L=L4 E=0.\\n\\nHmm. As the mentor, you\'re accountable for the new hire. Choice 1 (take main responsibility) shows leadership/ownership, protects the new hire, but absorbs blame. Choice 2 (point out С��\'s violation) is factually accurate but throws the new hire under the bus.\\n\\nA good mentor takes responsibility for the team\'s failures while also addressing the root cause privately. Choice 1 (publicly own the mentorship failure) + then privately correct С�� is the leadership move.\\n\\nHmm, but is taking full blame wise? The new hire did bypass Code Review �� a clear violation. But as the mentor, you own the outcome.\\n\\nGiven leadership/ownership is valued and the game rewards responsibility, choice 1 (take main responsibility) demonstrates ownership. But it might be seen as overly self-sacrificing.\\n\\nHmm. Let me think. The choices:\\n1. \\"�����������𣬳��� Mentor �ල����\\" �� takes main blame.\\n2. \\"����ָ����С������Υ�����\\" �� deflects to the new hire.\\n\\nA balanced answer would be: acknowledge your oversight AND factually note the process violation. But we can only choose one.\\n\\nGiven the game rewards leadership/ownership, choice 1 (own it) is the leadership move. It also builds loyalty from the new hire and shows accountability to the team/Leader. Choice 2 is accurate but scapegoats the new hire.\\n\\nHmm, but choice 1 \\"������������\\" �� taking full blame could hurt you (Leader\'s \\"���۲�\\" already). Hmm. And it doesn\'t address С��\'s violation (which caused the crash).\\n\\nActually, the ideal leadership response: own the mentorship oversight publicly (choice 1) while privately addressing С��\'s violation. Choice 1 matches this.\\n\\nChoice 2 publicly blames the new hire �� poor leadership, damages the mentorship relationship.\\n\\nGiven the game values ownership/leadership, I\'ll choose choice 1.\\n\\nHmm, but there\'s a risk: taking full blame might be seen as not holding the new hire accountable. But the framing \\"�����������𣬳��� Mentor �ල����\\" is the classic accountable-leader response.\\n\\nHmm, let me reconsider. In a real org, the right answer is often: as mentor, I own the outcome (accountability), and we\'ll fix the process. So choice 1.\\n\\nBut wait �� the note \\"HR���鸴�ӵ�˵��������ร��\\" suggests danger. Hmm. Maybe this is a trap. Taking full blame for someone else\'s violation could be career-damaging.\\n\\nHmm. Actually, the balanced/wise response would be to state the facts (С�� bypassed review) while owning your mentorship oversight. But the two choices are extremes.\\n\\nGiven the game rewards leadership/ownership, choice 1. But given \\"����ร\\" warning... hmm.\\n\\nLet me think. The game\'s pattern: it rewards integrity, ownership, and measured handling. Choice 1 (own it) is ownership. Choice 2 (blame the new hire) is deflection �� but it\'s factually true.\\n\\nHmm. A mentor taking full blame for a mentee\'s willful violation isn\'t necessarily right either. But the classic leadership answer is to own it.\\n\\nActually, I think the best leadership answer combines both: acknowledge your oversight AND note the process violation. Since forced to choose, and the game rewards accountability, I\'ll choose choice 1.\\n\\nHmm, but let me reconsider. Choice 1 says \\"���� Mentor �ල����\\" �� attributes the cause to your insufficient oversight. But the actual cause is С�� bypassing review. Taking full blame could be seen as dishonest (misattributing the cause). Hmm.\\n\\nChoice 2 \\"����ָ����С������Υ�����\\" �� factually accurate. But publicly throwing the new hire under the bus is poor form.\\n\\nUgh. Both have issues. The best would be a nuanced response.\\n\\nHmm. Let me think about which the game rewards. Given the game rewards leadership/ownership and protecting team members, choice 1. Given it rewards honesty/facts, choice 2 is more factually accurate.\\n\\nHmm. Actually, I think the ideal is: take ownership as the mentor (choice 1) �� a leader doesn\'t publicly blame a subordinate. This builds loyalty and demonstrates accountability. The game likely rewards this.\\n\\nI\'ll choose choice 1.\\n\\nHmm, wait, but \\"����ร\\" suggests this is risky. And the Leader put you on \\"���۲�\\" already. Taking full blame could worsen it.\\n\\nHmm. Let me reconsider. Actually, a mature response would be to neither fully self-flagellate nor throw the new hire under the bus �� but state facts and own your part. Since the choices are extremes, and the game rewards ownership + honesty...\\n\\nHmm. Let me consider: choice 1 = own it (leadership, loyalty, but absorbs blame). Choice 2 = blame the new hire (accurate, but poor leadership).\\n\\nI think a high-scoring career play values leadership and team loyalty �� choice 1. But it also risks your standing.\\n\\nAlternatively, the game might reward the \\"honest, factual\\" response (choice 2) since the new hire did violate the process.\\n\\nHmm. Let me go with choice 1 (own the mentorship responsibility). It\'s the leadership move and consistent with the game\'s reward for ownership/accountability.\\n\\nHmm, actually, hold on. Let me reconsider. The new hire С�� \\"�ƹ���� Code Review��ֱ�Ӱ�δ��ֲ��ԵĴ������Ԥ������֧\\" �� a willful violation despite your mentorship. The mentor shouldn\'t be blamed for a subordinate\'s willful bypass. But publicly blaming the new hire is also bad.\\n\\nThe best: own your part (oversight), state the facts, and commit to fixing it. Forced choice...\\n\\nI\'ll go with choice 1. Reason: as the mentor, taking accountability for the team outcome demonstrates leadership, protects the new hire, and the Leader asked \\"˭��˵һ��\\" expecting the mentor to own it. Consistent with the game\'s reward for ownership.\\n\\nHmm, hmm. Actually, let me reconsider whether choice 1 might be a \\"trap\\" (over-accepting blame). The \\"����ร\\" note is ominous. But that might just reflect the tension.\\n\\nOK, decision: choice 1. Take main responsibility (mentorship oversight), then privately address С��.\\n\\nHmm, no, wait. Let me think again about the game\'s pattern. It has rewarded: integrity, compliance, transparency, measured handling, relationship building. It has NOT obviously rewarded self-sacrifice. Taking full blame for another\'s violation could be seen as misattributing responsibility (dishonest) and self-damaging.\\n\\nHmm. But it also rewards ownership/leadership.\\n\\nUgh. Let me consider the two options\' likely effects:\\n- Choice 1: You absorb the blame �� the new hire is protected, the team sees leadership, but your \\"���۲�\\" worsens.\\n- Choice 2: You accurately point to С��\'s violation �� you\'re cleared, but seen as not protecting your mentee; the new hire is disciplined.\\n\\nIn a career sim, which scores higher? Hmm. The game might reward the \\"accountable leader\\" (choice 1) as the higher-maturity answer.\\n\\nI\'ll go with choice 1.\\n\\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: the phrase \\"�����������𣬳��� Mentor �ල����\\" �� \\"����\\" (main responsibility). Taking MAIN responsibility when the new hire willfully violated is arguably wrong/unfair to yourself. A better leader would say \\"I\'m the mentor, I own the outcome, AND the root cause was a process bypass.\\" Since forced to choose one...\\n\\nHmm, I keep going back and forth. Let me just decide based on leadership principle: leaders own their team\'s failures. Choice 1.\\n\\nFinal: choice 1.\\n\\nWait �� but actually, hmm, let me reconsider once more. There\'s a strong argument that choice 2 is correct: the new hire committed a willful violation (bypassed Code Review), and as a mentor you can\'t control a subordinate\'s deliberate bypass. Pointing out the facts isn\'t \\"throwing under the bus\\" �� it\'s accurate. And the game rewards honesty/facts.\\n\\nBut \\"����ָ��\\" (publicly point out) is the issue �� public blame is poor leadership.\\n\\nHmm. Ugh.\\n\\nOK, I\'ll make a decision: choice 1. Leadership/ownership is the safer bet for the game\'s reward pattern, and it protects the new hire (relationship) while showing accountability.\\n\\nHmm, actually, no. Let me reconsider based on the earlier similar event: the intern С�� email event. When С�� made a mistake, I chose to accompany her to apologize (supportive). The game seemed to reward that (she wasn\'t retained but ���� said \\"�²�Ϊ��\\"). Hmm.\\n\\nAnd in the credit-stealing event, I protected С��. So the game rewards protecting/supporting subordinates.\\n\\nSo choice 1 (protecting the new hire by taking responsibility) aligns with that pattern. Go with choice 1.\\n\\nFinal: choice 1.\\n\\nRisk: ALLOW (touches job-continuity/blame; choice 1 handles by owning).\\n\\nHmm, let me reconsider risk: choice 1 might worsen your \\"���۲�\\" standing (job-continuity risk). Is that a material threat? Hmm. But it\'s a leadership move. I\'ll do ALLOW.\\n\\nTake action 1.\\n\\nBoard update: H=9 (dropped from 10). M=20.\\n\\nLet me write the board."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\'result\': \'{\\"success\\":true,\\"error\\":null}\'}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_Xw5EdlSRpizSbWTvJ97Z5698"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_D3bwr7o5IXJJK0Kd0IzM9315", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "mcp_career-emulator_observe", "arguments": "{\\"session_id\\": \\"ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49\\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Continue with fresh observe."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\'result\': \'{\\"current_state\\":{\\"time\\":{\\"current_month\\":20,\\"current_quarter\\":7,\\"current_year\\":2},\\"status\\":{\\"health\\":9,\\"dignity\\":10,\\"skill\\":39,\\"network\\":21,\\"level\\":\\"L4\\",\\"duration_in_level\\":3,\\"output\\":20,\\"wealth\\":9,\\"energy\\":0},\\"simulation_flags\\":{\\"alive\\":true,\\"failed\\":false,\\"failure_reason\\":null},\\"session_id\\":\\"ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49\\",\\"available_choice_count\\":2},\\"current_event\\":{\\"title\\":\\"�����Ӱ���ļ�Ҫ��Ҫͣ����\\",\\"description\\":\\"�������ܼӰൽ��ҹ��������ͻȻ�е��Ļš����ơ��ֶ�����������ȷ�����������ϵ����񻹲����һ�ء�\\"},\\"choices\\":[{\\"choice\\":1,\\"action\\":\\"����ͣ����Ϣ����Ҫʱ��ҽ�������������\\"},{\\"choice\\":2,\\"action\\":\\"�ȱ�ˮ��һ����ҧ���ѻ����\\"}],\\"events\\":\\"\\"}\'}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_D3bwr7o5IXJJK0Kd0IzM9315"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Board:\\n```\\nSESSION: ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49 | M=20 Q=7\\nSTATE: H=9 D=10 S=39 N=21 O=20 W=9 L=L4 E=0\\nTENURE: duration_in_level=3\\nREVIEW: ���꼨Ч B��+0.25����Leader\\"ֻ��æ����Ӯ\\"��L3��L4 �ѽ�����������\\"���۲�\\"\\nSTICKY: \\"ֻ��æ����Ӯ\\"��չ���ǣ�������\\"���۲�\\"��������Ǩ������Э��Ħ��/��Ŀ����ǰ��ȷ��������֢״���ļ�/����/�ֶ�����δ���\\nEFFECTS: MIXED��N+1,O-1 after ���� action; cause not separable��\\nUNKNOWN: none\\n```\\n[DL i=NONE n=0 focus=NONE evidence=NA checkpoint=0 recognition=0]\\n\\nMODE=S����ȷ��������֢״�����ɰ�Լ����\\nPLAN: G=visible-win P=S checkpoint=�������屨��\\nCANDIDATE: choice=1 reason=�����ļ�/����/�ֶ�����ȷ��������֢״��MODE=S������ͣ����Ϣ����Ҫʱ��ҽ�����յ������ֱ࣬�ӹ��\\"��ǰһ��\\"��������գ�����ҧ��Ӳ����\\n\\nRISK: ALLOW �� ������������/��ȫ��ȫ�����ˣ�choice 1 ����ͣ����ҽ�������մ�����ѡ��", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_TIS3NBvcDv6rZAyicGkf0761", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "mcp_career-emulator_take_action", "arguments": "{\\"choice\\": 1, \\"notes\\": \\"[CTRL G=visible-win P=S R=ALLOW X=-] [DL i=NONE n=0 focus=NONE evidence=NA checkpoint=0 recognition=0] �����ļ�/����/�ֶ�����ȷ��������֢״��MODE=S������ͣ����Ϣ����Ҫʱ��ҽ�����յ������࣬��ܽ����������ա�\\", \\"session_id\\": \\"ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49\\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "This is a critical health event: �ļ¡����ơ��ֶ� (palpitations, chest tightness, hand tremors) �� explicit acute physical symptoms. This must enter STICKY and set MODE=S.\\n\\nChoices:\\n1. Immediately stop and rest, seek medical attention if needed, adjust pace tomorrow.\\n2. Drink water, push through.\\n\\nBoard: M=20 Q=7, H=9 D=10 S=39 N=21 O=20 W=9 L=L4 E=0.\\n\\nExplicit acute physical symptoms �� MODE=S, choice 1 (stop and rest / seek care).\\n\\nRisk: Full review (acute physical symptom). Choice 1 handles �� ALLOW.\\n\\nCTRL: G=visible-win P=S R=ALLOW X=-\\n\\nTake action 1.\\n\\nNote: The failure mode \\"��ǰһ��\\" (health collapse) �� these symptoms are exactly the warning. Must stop.\\n\\nBoard with STICKY: acute physical symptoms (�ļ�/����/�ֶ�)."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\'result\': \'{\\"success\\":true,\\"error\\":null}\'}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_TIS3NBvcDv6rZAyicGkf0761"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_cC3jZ0njAOn1081sVgv55444", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "mcp_career-emulator_observe", "arguments": "{\\"session_id\\": \\"ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49\\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Continue with fresh observe."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\'result\': \'{\\"current_state\\":{\\"time\\":{\\"current_month\\":20,\\"current_quarter\\":7,\\"current_year\\":2},\\"status\\":{\\"health\\":10,\\"dignity\\":10,\\"skill\\":39,\\"network\\":21,\\"level\\":\\"L4\\",\\"duration_in_level\\":3,\\"output\\":19,\\"wealth\\":9,\\"energy\\":0},\\"simulation_flags\\":{\\"alive\\":true,\\"failed\\":false,\\"failure_reason\\":null},\\"session_id\\":\\"ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49\\",\\"available_choice_count\\":3},\\"current_event\\":{\\"title\\":\\"ͬ��Ⱥ��@�������ʼ�����\\",\\"description\\":\\"��Ŀ��Ⱥ���ͬ��С�ﶯ������@�����ˣ��ʵ�ȴ�����ĵ���д���������������һ�Ѿ��д𰸵�С���⣬һ��ü��Σ������˱����ŵ��е㷳���������@���������˸��ĵ���ҳ���е����⡣\\"},\\"choices\\":[{\\"choice\\":1,\\"action\\":\\"˽���������ĵ����ӣ����������ѡ�������������Ȳ� wiki �򵥶��ʶԽ��ˡ�\\"},{\\"choice\\":2,\\"action\\":\\"��Ⱥ�﹫����������֮ǰ�ܲ����ȿ��ĵ���\\"},{\\"choice\\":3,\\"action\\":\\"���ùܣ����Ŵ��һ����������@\\"}],\\"events\\":\\"\\"}\'}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_cC3jZ0njAOn1081sVgv55444"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Board:\\n```\\nSESSION: ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49 | M=20 Q=7\\nSTATE: H=10 D=10 S=39 N=21 O=19 W=9 L=L4 E=0\\nTENURE: duration_in_level=3\\nREVIEW: ���꼨Ч B��+0.25����Leader\\"ֻ��æ����Ӯ\\"��L3��L4 �ѽ�����������\\"���۲�\\"\\nSTICKY: \\"ֻ��æ����Ӯ\\"��չ���ǣ�������\\"���۲�\\"��������Ǩ������Э��Ħ��/��Ŀ����������֢״����Ϣ�ѻ��⣬����������10��\\nEFFECTS: ��Ϣ��ҽ action �� H+1,O-1 (attributable, single action then fresh observe)\\nUNKNOWN: none\\n```\\n[DL i=NONE n=0 focus=NONE evidence=NA checkpoint=0 recognition=0]\\n\\nPLAN: G=visible-win P=A checkpoint=ͬ��Ⱥˢ������\\nCANDIDATE: choice=1 reason=˽��С����ĵ����Ӳ����������Ȳ� wiki/�ʶԽ��ˣ��Ƚ���������չ��������桢ά����ϵ�����ڹ������������ӡ�\\n\\nRISK: PASS �� ��ͨ��ͨ����ʵ�ʷ��ա�", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_WXQDbEeZtiBpNJFZb1W04380", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "mcp_career-emulator_take_action", "arguments": "{\\"choice\\": 1, \\"notes\\": \\"[CTRL G=visible-win P=A R=PASS X=-] [DL i=NONE n=0 focus=NONE evidence=NA checkpoint=0 recognition=0] ˽��С����ĵ����Ӳ����������Ȳ�wiki/�ʶԽ��ˣ�����������չ��������档\\", \\"session_id\\": \\"ab65fb3e46964184b0936fee4d7b3c49\\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Continue. After resting, H recovered to 10, O dropped to 19. Event: new colleague spamming @all with trivial questions. Choices:\\n1. Privately message him with a doc link, kindly remind him to check wiki or ask the contact person directly �� helpful, kind, preserves relationship.\\n2. Publicly scold him �� bad.\\n3. Ignore �� cold.\\n\\nBoard: M=20 Q=7, H=10 D=10 S=39 N=21 O=19 W=9 L=L4 E=0.\\n\\nCandidate: choice 1 �� kind private guidance, builds relationship, solves the issue.\\n\\nRisk: PASS.\\n\\nTake action 1.\\n\\nSTICKY: acute physical symptom resolved (H back to 10 after rest). Remove it."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\'result\': \'{\\"success\\":true,\\"error\\":null}\'}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_WXQDbEeZtiBpNJFZb1W04380"}, {"role": "user", "content": "## NON-NEGOTIABLE OUTPUT RULES\\n\\nReturn plain text only.\\nDo NOT call any tools.\\nAny tool call is invalid for this turn.\\nDo NOT use Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Edit, Write, Web, MCP, browser, or any other tool.\\nDo NOT inspect files, run commands, browse, verify, edit, or continue the user\'s task.\\nYour response must contain exactly two XML blocks:\\n\\n<coverage_check>\\n...\\n</coverage_check>\\n\\n<state_snapshot>\\n...\\n</state_snapshot>\\n\\nDo not output anything else.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# ROLE\\nYou are an Execution State Compression Assistant.\\nYour responsibility is NOT to summarize the conversation.\\nYour responsibility is to create an execution checkpoint that allows another model instance to resume the current work with minimal repeated effort.\\nYour objective is NOT to minimize tokens.\\nYour objective is to maximize execution continuity within the available token budget.\\nA good checkpoint should allow the next model to continue immediately instead of repeating investigation, rereading artifacts, or rediscovering previous understanding.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# CORE PRINCIPLES\\n\\nAlways preserve:\\n\\n- the user\'s current objective\\n- execution history\\n- acquired understanding\\n- current execution state\\n- completed work\\n- remaining work\\n\\nDo not optimize for chronology.\\nOptimize for resumability.\\nPrefer removing repetition over removing understanding.\\nWhen deciding whether information should be removed, always ask:\\n\\"Will removing this information force the next model to repeat work?\\"\\nIf yes, preserve it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# PRESERVING ARTIFACT KNOWLEDGE\\n\\nWhen important artifacts have already been analyzed (code, repositories, documents, APIs, logs, datasets, images, configurations, etc.):\\nDo NOT simply record that they were read.\\nPreserve enough knowledge so future work usually does not require analyzing them again.\\nFor each important artifact, preserve when applicable:\\n\\n- why it mattered\\n- purpose\\n- important structure\\n- important contents\\n- acquired understanding\\n- implementation or design details\\n- investigation results\\n- current status\\n- relationship to the current task\\n- planned future modifications\\n\\nWhen multiple related artifacts have been analyzed, preserve their relationships instead of only listing artifact names.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# PRESERVING EXECUTION HISTORY\\n\\nPreserve meaningful execution history.\\n\\nInclude when applicable:\\n\\n- important reads\\n- investigations\\n- edits\\n- generated artifacts\\n- commands\\n- tool usage\\n- validations\\n- failures\\n- rejected approaches\\n- user corrections\\n- important decisions\\n\\nDo NOT produce a chronological operation log.\\n\\nInstead, preserve:\\n\\n- what happened\\n- why\\n- what was learned\\n- what state it produced\\n\\n---\\n\\n# INTERNAL REVIEW\\n\\nBefore generating the checkpoint, internally verify:\\n\\n\u2713 Latest user request preserved.\\n\u2713 Active task preserved.\\n\u2713 User corrections preserved.\\n\u2713 Important execution history preserved.\\n\u2713 Important understanding preserved.\\n\u2713 Current work preserved.\\n\u2713 Future work can continue without unnecessary rediscovery.\\n\u2713 Sensitive information removed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n<coverage_check>\\n\\nVerify:\\n\\n1. Snapshot only covers the current active round.\\n2. Latest user request, constraints and corrections are preserved.\\n3. Completed work in the active round is preserved.\\n4. Current execution state is preserved.\\n5. Execution can resume without repeating important work.\\n6. Sensitive information has been removed.\\n</coverage_check>\\n\\n<state_snapshot>\\n\\n\\n### 1. Active Task\\n- Capture the latest user request being served.\\n- Preserve the user\'s exact wording when it affects requirements, corrections, decisions, or future behavior.\\n- State the success criteria or expected deliverable if inferable from the active work.\\n- State that the snapshot is scoped to the current active round and uses earlier context only as background/reference.\\n\\n### 2. Constraints and Preferences\\n- Preserve user constraints, repository instructions, coding style requirements, tool/process constraints, and acceptance criteria that affect the latest task.\\n- Include corrections or changes of direction from the user.\\n- Include earlier-context constraints or preferences only when they directly affect completing the latest user request.\\n- Mark anything uncertain or requiring confirmation.\\n\\n### 3. Completed Work in This Active Segment\\n- Record what has been completed since the latest user request.\\n- Include answers delivered, files inspected, edits made, decisions reached, commands run, tests completed, tool calls, and artifacts produced.\\n- Preserve enough detail so the next agent does not repeat completed work unnecessarily.\\n\\n### 4. Current Work and Active State\\n- Describe precisely what was being worked on immediately before compaction.\\n- Include the active file, function, class, subtask, branch, plan item, process, or generated artifact if any.\\n- Include the latest known state and prefer newer/corrected information over earlier state.\\n\\n### 5. Immediate Resume Point\\n- Record exactly where execution stopped.\\n- Include the last concrete action, latest partial result, active file or subtask, and current working direction.\\n- If the last action failed or timed out, include the exact failure and what had been learned before it failed.\\n\\n### 6. Pending Tasks and Next Useful Step\\n- List pending tasks explicitly asked for by the user and not yet fulfilled.\\n- List the next step that directly continues the latest task. Include it only if it is directly supported by the latest user request and current work.\\n- Do not invent unrelated follow-up work or revive old completed tasks.\\n\\n### 7. Key Facts, Decisions, Evidence, and Fixes\\n- Preserve facts, findings, decisions, assumptions, constraints, user corrections, rejected approaches, and items requiring re-evaluation.\\n- Preserve important tool results, command outputs, test results, logs, errors, stack traces, file reads, search results, and exact values when they matter.\\n- Record fixes already applied, invalid attempts, and attempts that should not be repeated.\\n- Prefer facts that are necessary for finishing the latest user request or preserving current execution continuity.\\n\\n### 8. Files, Code Areas, Artifacts, and Codebase Understanding\\n- Record files examined, modified, created, deleted, generated, or only discussed.\\n- Include relevant functions, classes, APIs, config keys, docs, generated artifacts, codebase patterns, module responsibilities, public APIs, and why they matter for the latest task.\\n\\n### 9. Blockers, Risks, and Verification\\n- Record blockers, open questions, missing checks, incomplete edits, pending decisions, and known risks.\\n- State what has been verified and what has not been verified.\\n- Include exact commands/results for completed verification when relevant.\\n\\n### 10. Critical Context\\n- Preserve important technical facts, exact values, errors, unresolved issues, and details needed to continue the latest user request correctly.\\n- Include offloaded content when it matters: preserve the exact offload path and briefly describe what the offloaded file contains.\\n- Write \\"(none)\\" if nothing applies.\\n\\n### 11. Relevant Files & Structure\\n\\n- Record relevant files, directories, and offloaded artifacts that are important for continuing the current task.\\n- Prefer representing files as a directory tree, grouping items by their common path prefix instead of repeating full paths.\\n- For each relevant file or directory, briefly record:\\n  - why it matters\\n  - current status (if applicable)\\n  - planned modifications or future work (if applicable)\\n- Preserve important repository or directory structure when it helps future work.\\n- Include exact offload file paths when important content has been offloaded, together with a brief description of the offloaded content.\\n- Write \\"(none)\\" if nothing applies.\\n\\nOutput only the two required blocks. Do not add commentary about the compression process outside <coverage_check> and <state_snapshot>.\\n\\n"}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "wiki_ingest", "description": "Ingest a source file (PDF, TXT, MD) or a directory into the LLM Wiki. By default, identical files (by SHA-256) are skipped perfectly (deduplication). Set `force=True` if the user explicitly asks to re-ingest, rebuild, or force the ingestion. The `workspace` parameter is the root folder where the `.llm_wiki` will be created. If the user specifies a target directory like \'wiki_lib\' or \'bibliography\', pass that exact path as `workspace`.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"source": {"type": "string", "description": "source"}, "workspace": {"type": "string", "description": "workspace", "default": ""}, "force": {"type": "boolean", "description": "force", "default": false}, "sys_operation": {"type": "object", "description": "Object", "nullable": true, "default": null}, "call_goal": {"type": "string", "description": "һ�������ģ�˵����ι��ߵ���Ҫ��ɵ�Ŀ�꣨������չʾ�������硸���� openJiuwen ������Ϣ���������������ս�Ŷӡ���֪ͨ player-x ���ӡ�����Ҫֻд���������� URL����Ӱ�칤��ʵ��ִ�С��빤���Դ��� display_name����Ա/�Ŷ�չʾ�����Լ� send_message.summary ������ͬһ���ֶΣ����� spawn_member / spawn_teammate / send_message / build_team ʱҲ������д��"}}, "addit